"""Original fictional IQ training case; no executed GMP records or approvals."""
import json
from pathlib import Path
L=[]
def add(title,objective,scenes,activity,model,worked,quiz):
 L.append(dict(id=len(L)+1,title=title,objective=objective,scenes=[dict(title=t,labels=labels,narration=n,description=d) for t,labels,n,d in scenes],activity=activity,model=model,worked=worked,quiz=dict(question=quiz[0],options=quiz[1],answer=quiz[2],feedback=quiz[3])))
add('What installation qualification establishes','Distinguish installed configuration from operating performance.',[
('From design to installation',['Approved design','Installed configuration','Documented comparison'],'Welcome to Module Four, Installation Qualification, or I Q. We continue with the fictional V-201 oral-liquid mixing vessel. The design review described what was intended. Now the team compares the installed system with the accepted design and manufacturer recommendations. Every identifier and observation in this module is invented for training. No real equipment has been qualified.','The design baseline and installed system feed one comparison; neither input alone establishes conformity.'),
('Three different evidence questions',['DQ: proposed design','IQ: installed system','OQ: challenged operation'],'A drawing shows a temperature sensor connection. A walkdown identifies which sensor is installed at that connection. An operational challenge examines how the assembled loop behaves. These answer different questions. Finding the correct tag does not demonstrate alarm response, and a successful alarm test does not repair missing material identification. Keep each conclusion tied to the evidence actually obtained.','The three stages form a sequence of different evidence questions, not three interchangeable approvals.'),
('Set the system boundary',['Vessel and components','Utility interfaces','Control-system interfaces'],'For our case, the installation boundary includes the vessel, agitator, outlet components, instruments and defined utility connections. The upstream utility plant has its own qualification scope. Record where one system ends and another begins. A qualified water system does not automatically prove that a new branch has the correct connection or that its delivered water is suitable.','The vessel boundary includes its connections; upstream utility qualification remains a separate linked scope.'),
('Limit the conclusion',['Installation evidence','Open findings','Next-stage decision'],'The teaching conclusion is limited to installation evidence reviewed against the defined baseline. It is not permission to manufacture a batch. Operational and performance verification remain separate needs. Where stages are combined in an approved approach, the underlying evidence questions still remain visible. Your first activity separates installation observations from claims that require later studies.','Installation findings feed a bounded conclusion and a next-stage decision; routine production is outside this diagram.')],
'Classify: sensor serial matches schedule; alarm stops heating; batch blend is uniform. Explain the evidence boundary.',
'The first is installation identity evidence. Alarm response needs a defined operational challenge. Blend uniformity needs a justified performance/process study; neither is proven by identity alone.',
[['Observation','Supported conclusion'],['Sensor serial matches schedule','Installed identity matches this baseline'],['Alarm challenge not executed','No alarm-performance conclusion'],['No product study','No blend-uniformity conclusion']],
('Which finding is directly an installation identity check?',['Alarm response under simulated sensor failure','Sensor serial matches the controlled instrument schedule','Blend uniformity across the batch'],1,'The serial comparison addresses installed identity; the other choices require functional or performance evidence.'))
add('Prepare the IQ baseline and protocol','Define what will be checked, how, and against which controlled criteria.',[
('Reconcile the input package',['URS and DQ outcome','Drawings and specifications','Approved IQ protocol'],'The fictional installation package contains the user requirements, design-review outcome, accepted drawings and an installation protocol. List identifiers and revisions before the walkdown. An email attachment labelled final is not enough to establish its approval status. If the valve drawing changed after design review, identify the change and its accepted impact before relying on the old conclusion.','Requirements, design decisions and controlled drawings support the IQ protocol; revision gaps remain visible.'),
('Write an executable check',['Criterion and source','Method and instrument','Observation and reference'],'Replace verify installation is satisfactory with a check another trained person can repeat. For example, compare the installed outlet valve tag and model against schedule revision C, using a safe visual inspection and the identified manufacturer plate. Define what information will be recorded and how inaccessible markings will be handled. Do not leave the acceptance rule to be invented after seeing the result.','A criterion links to a method and an evidence field; vague satisfactory wording has no defined comparator.'),
('Check readiness safely',['Trained personnel','Safe access and isolation','Suitable test equipment'],'Before inspecting, confirm training, access arrangements and any required isolation with the responsible engineering team. Do not remove guards, open electrical panels or disconnect a process line merely to complete a checklist. If a marking cannot be reached safely, record that limitation and arrange an approved method. Schedule pressure does not turn an unsafe inspection into acceptable evidence.','Training, safe access and suitable tools are readiness inputs; this is not an isolation procedure.'),
('Resolve a missing criterion',['No approved dimension','Record the gap','Resolve before evaluation'],'Suppose the drawing shows a clearance but provides no approved minimum. Measuring a convenient distance and then calling it acceptable creates a circular argument. Raise the missing requirement to the responsible design and quality functions. The team may collect a clearly identified observation, but acceptance of that feature remains unresolved until the criterion and approach are properly established.','A missing clearance criterion blocks its acceptance decision; an observed dimension is not a substitute requirement.')],
'Write an IQ check for outlet valve identity with baseline, criterion, safe method and evidence fields.',
'Name the controlled schedule and revision, required tag/model, visual method, actual observed identifiers, evidence reference, executor/date and discrepancy route. Confirm safe access; do not infer acceptance from an unapproved drawing.',
[['Field','Teaching entry'],['Baseline','VAL-V201 revision C, fictional accepted schedule'],['Criterion','Tag/model match specified item'],['Observation','Actual tag/model and location recorded'],['Exception','Unreadable plate: identity unresolved']],
('The protocol has no criterion for a clearance measurement. What is defensible?',['Resolve the missing criterion before deciding acceptance','Use the measured clearance as the acceptance limit','Accept it because the supplier installed it'],0,'A result cannot define its own acceptance criterion.'))
add('Conduct a physical installation walkdown','Match the actual arrangement to controlled installation evidence.',[
('Start at the equipment plate',['Tag V-201','Model and serial','Installed location'],'The walkdown starts with equipment identity, not a copied supplier checklist. Compare the vessel tag, manufacturer model, serial number and location with the controlled package. A matching tag attached to a different vessel would not establish identity. Record discrepancies individually so a later reviewer can tell which identifiers matched and which remained uncertain.','A vessel plate contains separate tag, model and serial fields; the comparison includes physical location.'),
('Trace components in place',['Agitator and support','Valve orientation','Instrument connection'],'Work through the installed arrangement systematically. Identify the agitator assembly and supports, then trace the outlet and instrument connections against the relevant drawing. Confirm required orientation and accessible features using the approved method. Do not run the agitator to infer installation conformity when the authorized activity is a static inspection. Functional rotation checks belong in the defined test plan.','Component locations are checked around the vessel; static observations are distinct from energised functional tests.'),
('Inspect the removal envelope',['Instrument body','Removal direction','Adjacent pipe rack'],'The design allowed a rear instrument to slide out for maintenance, but the installed pipe rack crosses that removal envelope. A front-facing photograph misses this problem. Compare the full maintenance envelope with the approved arrangement and manufacturer requirements. No universal clearance is supplied here. Document the obstruction and its consequences without inventing a numerical GMP minimum.','Plan view: the dashed instrument-removal path intersects the adjacent pipe rack; no minimum clearance is implied.'),
('Record what was inaccessible',['Check not completed','Reason and location','Planned verification'],'A covered connection cannot be recorded as verified solely because its neighbour matches. Record the inaccessible item, why it could not be examined and the agreed follow-up. If suitable controlled evidence supports another verification method, justify its applicability. Distinguish direct observation from document review so the evidence does not appear stronger than it is.','An inaccessible item remains an explicit incomplete check with a follow-up, rather than inheriting a neighbour’s result.')],
'Write a finding for a rear instrument blocked by a pipe rack. Separate observation, criterion and impact.',
'Record the location and obstruction, cite the approved removal envelope/manufacturer instruction, identify maintenance implications, assign an action and specify reinspection. Do not invent a clearance value.',
[['Element','Example'],['Observation','Pipe rack crosses rear instrument removal path'],['Comparator','Accepted layout and removal instruction'],['Impact','Instrument cannot be removed as intended'],['Verification','Reinspect corrected arrangement against baseline']],
('An inaccessible connection is next to one that matches the drawing. What is its status?',['Accepted by similarity with the visible connection','Accepted because the supplier signed the drawing','Not yet verified unless an applicable alternative is justified'],2,'Each conclusion needs applicable evidence; adjacency is not verification.'))
add('Verify materials and component traceability','Link the installed contact path to applicable material evidence.',[
('Map the contact components',['Vessel and impeller','Gaskets and valve seats','Outlet and hose'],'Follow the product-contact path through the fictional vessel. Include the agitator, gaskets, valve seats and transfer connection, not only the metal shell. The installation check asks whether the specified materials are actually represented by the installed components and their evidence. A certificate for the vessel body does not identify an elastomer fitted later.','The contact path contains metal and non-metal components; the vessel-shell certificate covers only its linked scope.'),
('Connect item to certificate',['Installed identifier','Supplier traceability','Certificate scope'],'A certificate says material grade X, but which installed item does it describe? Use the applicable identifiers and controlled supplier traceability to link the record to the component. The precise method depends on the component and project. Do not require one universal certificate format or assume every gasket has a readable individual serial number. Evaluate the traceability actually available.','The installed component connects through an applicable lot or other identifier to the certificate’s stated scope.'),
('Treat missing links as gaps',['Gasket lot unresolved','Body certificate available','Gasket evidence pending'],'In our example, the vessel certificate is available but the installed gasket lot cannot be linked to its material record. Keep the gasket finding open. The vessel certificate does not fill that gap. Obtain applicable traceability, or use an authorized corrective approach followed by verification. Changing a label without evidence would only conceal the uncertainty.','A solid link covers the vessel body; a broken link marks the unresolved gasket evidence.'),
('Keep suitability and identity separate',['Specified material','Installed material evidence','Compatibility rationale'],'Material identity and material suitability are related but different questions. The design review should have considered intended exposure, while installation evidence identifies what was fitted. If an authorized substitution changes the material, assess the compatibility and qualification implications through change control. Do not declare cleaning effectiveness solely because the installed material matches a certificate.','Matching identity supports the installation comparison but does not replace compatibility or cleaning-performance assessment.')],
'Build a three-row contact-material register and describe how to handle a missing gasket traceability link.',
'Include body, gasket and outlet component; record specification, installed identifier, evidence reference and status. Keep the gasket unresolved, obtain applicable evidence or authorized correction, and reassess any material change.',
[['Component','Evidence status'],['Vessel body','Applicable certificate linked to vessel'],['Gasket','Lot-to-material link missing; unresolved'],['Outlet valve','Part identifier linked to accepted specification']],
('The body certificate is available but gasket identity is unresolved. What follows?',['Accept both because they share the product path','Keep the gasket gap open and obtain applicable evidence','Use the body grade as the gasket material'],1,'Certificate scope does not extend automatically to other contact components.'))
add('Verify piping and utility interfaces','Check the installed connection while preserving upstream qualification boundaries.',[
('Trace source to point of use',['Utility source','Branch and isolation','Vessel connection'],'Trace the installed branch from its defined source to the vessel connection against the controlled piping diagram. Check the specified route, isolation and identifiers using safe methods. Do not assume a pipe has the intended contents because its colour looks familiar. A label and the actual connection must agree with the system information.','The utility source, branch isolation and vessel connection form a continuous traceable route.'),
('Read labels and direction',['Contents identified','Flow direction where needed','Drawing correspondence'],'For fixed pipework, contents identification and applicable flow direction help prevent mistakes. Compare labels with the route and controlled drawing, including places where lines cross. A correct label on the wrong connected branch remains a discrepancy. Record the location so the engineering team can resolve the actual connection rather than merely replacing the label.','Pipe identification is compared with the actual route; arrows represent stated direction, not proof of correct connection.'),
('Separate connection from quality',['Installation identity','Utility qualification status','Point-of-use evidence'],'The branch may be installed as drawn while the required utility-quality evidence is still unavailable. Record those as separate statuses. A pressure indication alone cannot establish chemical or microbiological suitability. Determine what qualification and sampling evidence applies to the intended use through the approved project approach, rather than borrowing limits from an unrelated sterile process.','A correct physical connection and adequate utility-quality evidence are independent inputs to readiness.'),
('Assess a changed branch',['New connection installed','Scope and impact assessed','Verification defined'],'A late branch addition may affect the upstream system as well as the receiving vessel. Identify the changed configuration and assess the required installation and other verification before use. Do not assume that the original utility qualification covers every future extension. Our workbook asks which records and responsible system owners must be linked before closing the interface.','A new branch triggers scope and impact assessment on both sides of the interface.')],
'List separate evidence for a correctly installed utility connection and suitable delivered utility quality.',
'Installation evidence includes route, isolation, labels and drawing comparison. Quality evidence follows the approved utility qualification and relevant point-of-use requirements. Cross-reference both; neither substitutes for the other.',
[['Question','Evidence type'],['Correct branch connected?','Walkdown against controlled diagram'],['Utility suitable for use?','Applicable qualification and quality evidence'],['New branch covered?','Change impact and defined verification']],
('The pressure gauge reads normally. What is still unresolved?',['Chemical and microbiological suitability of the utility','Whether the gauge has any readable indication','Whether the observation can be recorded'],0,'A pressure reading does not establish utility quality.'))
add('Review instruments and calibration evidence','Check identity, range, calibration relevance and the effect of discrepancies.',[
('Identify the installed chain',['Sensor identity','Transmitter configuration','Display and tag'],'Trace the installed measurement chain from sensing element to displayed value. Record the relevant identifiers, locations, ranges and configuration against the approved schedule. A display tag does not prove which sensor feeds it. Installation evidence provides the configuration baseline for later assembled-loop checks; it does not by itself establish accuracy throughout operation.','Sensor, transmitter and display are separate links in the installed chain, each with configuration evidence.'),
('Read beyond the sticker',['Applicable instrument','Range and test points','Results and status'],'A calibration sticker is a starting point for locating the record. Review whether the certificate identifies the correct instrument and whether its range, points, method, results and status support the intended check. Consider uncertainty and the applicable decision rule where relevant. A current date on an unrelated instrument certificate cannot justify using the installed device.','The certificate review compares identity, coverage and results; a due-date label alone is insufficient.'),
('Compare the observed error',['Reference: 40.0 °C','Indication: 40.4 °C','Error: +0.4 °C'],'For this fictional arithmetic exercise, the reference is forty point zero degrees Celsius and the indication is forty point four. Indication minus reference gives plus zero point four degrees. With a teaching limit of plus or minus zero point five, the magnitude is within that simple limit. This is not a full calibration acceptance decision; uncertainty, method and the approved decision rule still matter.','The calculation panel compares 40.0 °C reference with 40.4 °C indication; the displayed +0.4 °C is signed error, not uncertainty.'),
('Handle an unsuitable instrument',['Status discrepancy','Affected evidence review','Authorized correction'],'If an instrument is outside its required calibration status, identify which checks relied on it and assess their validity. Do not simply obtain a new certificate and assume earlier readings remain valid. The investigation may require additional evidence or repeated checks under an approved approach. Record the basis for the decision and retain the original observations.','A calibration-status discrepancy leads to review of affected evidence before correction and any repeat verification.')],
'Calculate indication error for 40.4 °C versus 40.0 °C, then identify information needed beyond the arithmetic.',
'Error is +0.4 °C, within the fictional ±0.5 °C magnitude limit. Confirm instrument identity, applicable method/range, uncertainty, decision rule and calibration status before concluding suitability.',
[['Quantity','Fictional value'],['Reference','40.0 °C'],['Indication','40.4 °C'],['Signed error','+0.4 °C'],['Simple limit','±0.5 °C; arithmetic only']],
('A calibration certificate belongs to a different serial number. What is defensible?',['Accept it if the due date is current','Accept it if both instruments have the same range','Resolve identity before relying on that certificate'],2,'Matching range and dates do not establish certificate applicability.'))
add('Assemble supplier and configuration records','Make the installed baseline maintainable without confusing documentation with functional validation.',[
('Collect usable instructions',['Operating instructions','Maintenance requirements','Installed model match'],'Collect the supplier instructions and maintenance requirements applicable to the installed model and revision. A large folder is not necessarily a usable handover. Check that the team can identify the correct document and that referenced drawings or schedules are available. Superseded manuals should not silently become the basis for maintaining the installed configuration.','Operating and maintenance records are linked to the installed model and revision, rather than counted as pages.'),
('Record control configuration',['Hardware identity','Software version','Controlled configuration'],'For the vessel control system, identify the relevant hardware, software version and configuration baseline through the approved approach. A version screenshot can support identification, but it does not establish that permissions, audit trails, alarms or backup restoration work adequately. Plan the applicable computerised-system validation separately; this introductory installation module does not claim complete Annex Eleven compliance.','The configuration baseline is an input to validation; version identification is not proof of functional controls.'),
('Connect maintenance readiness',['Applicable task','Responsible owner','Required resources'],'Link the supplier maintenance requirements to responsible site functions and the resources needed for the next authorized activity. For our case, a rear instrument requires a specific removal tool and accessible space. Record missing resources and their implications. Do not describe a task as established merely because a supplier manual mentions it.','Maintenance requirements connect to an owner and required resources; unresolved readiness remains visible.'),
('Review supplier test reuse',['Original evidence','Configuration applicability','Transport impact'],'A supplier test may be useful when its method, data and configuration are applicable and its reuse is justified. Consider what transport and installation could have changed. A factory photograph cannot prove that a field connection was installed correctly after shipment. Retain the rationale for what is reused and the site verification still required.','Supplier evidence passes through an applicability and transport-impact review before supporting a site conclusion.')],
'Prepare a handover index for instructions, configuration and supplier evidence, including one limitation.',
'Index document identifier, revision, installed-model applicability, location and owner. Explain that a software-version record establishes identity only; define separate functional validation. Justify any supplier evidence reuse.',
[['Record','What it supports'],['Applicable manual','Operating and maintenance information'],['Version/configuration record','Installed baseline identity'],['Factory test','Only justified, still-applicable evidence'],['Site connection check','Installed interface verification']],
('A software-version screenshot is attached. What does it support by itself?',['Identification of the observed software version','Adequacy of all access and audit-trail controls','Successful backup restoration under failure'],0,'Version identity is not functional validation.'))
add('Execute and record defensible observations','Preserve the relationship between criteria, observations and original evidence.',[
('Capture the actual result',['Criterion reference','Observed identifier','Executor and date'],'An installation record should make the comparison understandable. For the valve identity check, record the actual model and tag, the referenced schedule and the outcome. A tick without the required observation may leave a reviewer unable to reconstruct the decision. Use the approved form and record the work when performed, not from memory at the end of the week.','A check row connects its criterion to an actual observation and attributable execution details.'),
('Use photographs with context',['Location and subject','Evidence reference','Record relationship'],'A photograph can support an observation if the relevant feature is readable and its relationship to the check is clear. Record location, subject and the controlled evidence reference. A cropped image of a plate without the equipment context may be ambiguous. Photographs supplement the specified verification method; they are not automatically sufficient for every installation characteristic.','A contextual image and its evidence reference link back to a particular check and equipment item.'),
('Correct without obscuring',['Original remains readable','Correction attributed','Reason where appropriate'],'Suppose a handwritten serial number contains a transposed digit. Correct it using the approved documentation procedure, preserving the original entry and recording the required attribution and date, with a reason where appropriate. Do not erase the original or backdate the correction. For electronic records, use the controlled system process rather than an untracked replacement file.','The original entry remains traceable beside an attributed correction; an opaque overwrite is excluded.'),
('Explain incomplete fields',['Not applicable with basis','Not checked with reason','Unresolved action'],'Not applicable, not checked and failed are different statuses. A check outside the approved system scope needs a justified not-applicable basis. An inaccessible feature is not checked. A verified mismatch is a discrepancy. Keeping these statuses distinct prevents a completed-looking form from implying that evidence exists where it does not.','Three different statuses retain separate meanings and must not be collapsed into a single completion tick.')],
'Rewrite “all items OK” as an evidence-linked valve check, then explain an uninspected hidden connection.',
'Record actual tag/model, location, comparator revision and evidence reference with execution details. Mark the hidden connection not checked with reason and follow-up; do not call it not applicable just to complete the form.',
[['Status','Meaning in the exercise'],['Matched','Observation meets stated criterion'],['Not checked','No applicable verification completed'],['Not applicable','Justified exclusion from scope'],['Mismatch','Observed result differs from criterion']],
('A hidden connection could not be inspected. Which entry is accurate?',['Not applicable because access was difficult','Not checked, with reason and follow-up','Passed because no defect was visible'],1,'Lack of access does not demonstrate conformity or justify a scope exclusion.'))
add('Investigate discrepancies and manage changes','Keep failed checks, corrective work and re-verification traceable.',[
('Describe the mismatch',['Required valve model A','Observed valve model B','Affected function identified'],'The accepted schedule specifies valve model A, while the installed plate shows model B. Record the mismatch and its exact location. Do not decide from the model number alone whether the difference is harmless. Assess relevant materials, connection, cleaning and functional implications with the responsible experts. Retain the original observation even if the supplier later replaces the valve.','Required model A and observed model B form a recorded mismatch, with impact still to be assessed.'),
('Choose a controlled action',['Investigate cause and impact','Correct or assess change','Define verification'],'Correction may restore the specified valve, or a proposed design change may require an authorized assessment. These are not the same route. Do not revise the acceptance criterion merely to match what arrived. Define what verification follows the approved action and whether earlier checks or later tests are affected. Keep the decision and supporting rationale linked to the finding.','A finding branches into correction or authorized change, then returns to defined verification.'),
('Verify closure evidence',['Action completed','Applicable reinspection','Reviewed conclusion'],'A purchase order for the replacement is not closure evidence. After the authorized action, obtain the specified installation evidence and assess any related implications. Retain the original failure and the subsequent result as separate records. A clean second result should not erase the history or imply that the first configuration had always complied.','Action completion and reinspection feed a reviewed closure decision; ordering a part alone leaves the finding open.'),
('Limit conditional progression',['Defined next activity','No significant impact basis','Responsible authorization'],'Conditional progression is specific to the next qualification activity and requires a documented no-significant-impact assessment and responsible authorization. For example, an unresolved gasket issue may prevent wetted-system challenges even if a separately assessed dry control-display check could proceed. This is an illustration, not permission. Do not use a high completion percentage as a substitute for the assessment.','The open finding limits affected tests; a separate next-stage activity needs its own assessed and authorized basis.')],
'Draft a discrepancy entry for the wrong valve and identify what is required before closure or limited progression.',
'Record expected/actual model and evidence, assess impact, use the controlled correction/change route, define reinspection and retain history. Any limited OQ progression needs an activity-specific no-significant-impact assessment and authorization.',
[['Event','Status'],['Wrong valve observed','Deviation recorded and impact assessed'],['Replacement ordered','Action planned; finding still open'],['Replacement verified','Closure evidence available for review'],['Next-stage activity','Separate documented authorization required']],
('The replacement valve has been ordered. Is the installation finding closed?',['Yes, because purchasing accepted the action','Yes, because the supplier promised the correct model','No, applicable correction and verification evidence are still needed'],2,'An order is a planned action, not evidence of installed correction.'))
add('Report IQ and hand over to OQ','Summarize evidence, limitations and authorized next steps without overstating readiness.',[
('Reconcile the evidence index',['Executed checks','Attachments and findings','Controlled baseline'],'Before reporting, reconcile the executed checks with the evidence index and finding register. Missing attachments, ambiguous revisions and unexplained blanks can change the interpretation of a result. In our fictional package, the team confirms which checks are supported and which remain unresolved. This review does not create evidence; it tests whether the existing record supports the proposed conclusion.','Executed checks, attachments and findings converge on the report against one controlled baseline.'),
('Do not average away a gap',['Nine of ten supported','One material gap open','Significance governs decision'],'Nine of ten checks have supporting evidence, giving ninety percent numerical coverage. The remaining item is the unresolved gasket-material link. A high percentage does not make that gap acceptable. Evaluate its significance and the proposed next activity. The course practice score and this coverage calculation are learning aids, not regulatory release thresholds.','Nine supported cells and one unresolved cell illustrate 90% coverage; the unresolved material gap remains decision-relevant.'),
('Write a bounded recommendation',['Scope and evidence','Deviations and restrictions','Defined OQ handover'],'The recommendation should identify the installed scope reviewed, the evidence baseline, deviations and any restrictions. State the specific next-stage activity recommended for authorization rather than ready for production. Transfer the configuration records and unresolved dependencies to the operational-qualification team so their tests use the right system and do not assume a finding has disappeared.','A bounded IQ report passes configuration and restrictions to OQ; routine-use authorization is not represented.'),
('Defend the handover',['Trace a conclusion','Explain an open finding','Identify the authorized step'],'For the capstone, trace one conclusion back to its criterion, observation and evidence. Explain the remaining gasket finding and identify which proposed activities depend on it. Then draft a recommendation for the responsible reviewers. This self-study exercise is not independent competence certification, an executed qualification package or a substitute for site procedures and qualified human review.','The learner traces evidence, explains a limitation and recommends a bounded next step for human review.')],
'Prepare a short IQ handover with scope, evidence references, open gasket finding, restrictions and proposed OQ decision.',
'State the baseline and supported installation checks, retain the gasket gap, hold dependent wetted tests and request a documented decision for any separate activity. Include owners and evidence locations; 90% coverage does not authorize progression.',
[['Handover element','Teaching example'],['Coverage','9 of 10 checks supported = 90%'],['Open finding','Gasket material traceability unresolved'],['Restriction','Dependent wetted challenges held'],['Recommendation','Responsible review of defined OQ readiness']],
('Nine checks are supported and one important material gap remains. What governs progression?',['The significance of the gap and authorized next activity','The fact that coverage exceeds the course practice score','The number of pages already signed'],0,'Coverage does not replace impact assessment and authorization.'))
# Evidence-map descriptions correspond to the actual code-drawn image, not implied physical drawings.
MAPS=[[['DESIGN', 'INSTALLED', 'COMPARE'], ['DRAWING', 'WALKDOWN', 'CHALLENGE'], ['UPSTREAM', 'INTERFACE', 'VESSEL'], ['OBSERVED', 'UNRESOLVED', 'RECOMMEND']], [['URS / DQ', 'DRAWINGS', 'PROTOCOL'], ['REQUIREMENT', 'METHOD', 'RECORD'], ['TRAINING', 'SAFE ACCESS', 'TOOLS'], ['MEASURED', 'NO CRITERION', 'UNRESOLVED']], [['TAG', 'MODEL / SERIAL', 'LOCATION'], ['AGITATOR', 'OUTLET', 'SENSOR'], ['INSTRUMENT', 'PATH', 'PIPE RACK'], ['INACCESSIBLE', 'NOT CHECKED', 'FOLLOW-UP']], [['BODY', 'GASKET', 'OUTLET'], ['COMPONENT', 'LOT / ID', 'CERTIFICATE'], ['BODY: LINKED', 'GASKET: GAP', 'EVIDENCE NEEDED'], ['SPECIFIED', 'FITTED', 'SUITABILITY']], [['SOURCE', 'ISOLATION', 'CONNECTION'], ['CONTENTS', 'DIRECTION', 'ACTUAL ROUTE'], ['CONNECTED', 'QUALITY DATA', 'READINESS'], ['OLD SYSTEM', 'NEW BRANCH', 'IMPACT REVIEW']], [['SENSOR', 'TRANSMITTER', 'DISPLAY'], ['IDENTITY', 'COVERAGE', 'RESULTS'], ['40.0 °C', '40.4 °C', '+0.4 °C'], ['STATUS GAP', 'AFFECTED DATA', 'FOLLOW-UP']], [['INSTALLED MODEL', 'APPLICABLE MANUAL', 'MAINTENANCE'], ['HARDWARE', 'VERSION', 'CONFIGURATION'], ['TASK', 'OWNER', 'RESOURCES'], ['FACTORY DATA', 'IMPACT REVIEW', 'SITE EVIDENCE']], [['CRITERION', 'OBSERVATION', 'ATTRIBUTION'], ['SUBJECT', 'LOCATION', 'CHECK REFERENCE'], ['ORIGINAL', 'CORRECTION', 'ATTRIBUTION'], ['NOT APPLICABLE', 'NOT CHECKED', 'MISMATCH']], [['REQUIRED: A', 'OBSERVED: B', 'IMPACT UNKNOWN'], ['INVESTIGATE', 'AUTHORIZE ACTION', 'VERIFY'], ['ORDERED ≠ CLOSED', 'REINSPECT', 'REVIEW'], ['OPEN FINDING', 'DEFINED ACTIVITY', 'AUTHORIZATION']], [['CHECKS', 'ATTACHMENTS', 'REPORT'], ['9 SUPPORTED', '1 OPEN', 'NO RELEASE RULE'], ['SCOPE', 'RESTRICTIONS', 'OQ HANDOVER'], ['TRACE EVIDENCE', 'EXPLAIN GAP', 'RECOMMEND']]]
for l in L:
 for j,scene in enumerate(l['scenes']):
  if (l['id'],j) not in [(3,2),(6,2),(10,1)]:
   scene['description']=scene['title']+': the evidence map groups '+', '.join(MAPS[l['id']-1][j])+'. These three related review topics correspond to the narrated explanation; the map is not a physical equipment drawing.'
Path(__file__).with_name('content.json').write_text(json.dumps({'title':'Installation Qualification (IQ)','lessons':L},indent=2,ensure_ascii=False)+'\n')
print('Created 10 lessons / 40 original scenes')
